# Rendered from this template by entrypoint.sh, which substitutes __GATEWAY_IP__
# with the address compose assigns to the gateway on the internal bridge.
#
# Why bind to that one address and not 0.0.0.0: this container now has an
# interface on the external bridge that reaches the internet. Ports bound to
# 0.0.0.0 would listen there too. Nothing outside the stack can reach them
# (they are never published, and only the gateway sits on the external bridge),
# but a proxy on a dark-web lab should listen on exactly the interface that is
# meant to reach it — here, the internal one — and nowhere else.
#
# The lab needs three ports beyond the stock SOCKS proxy, so traffic can be
# forced through Tor without each application cooperating:
#
#   SocksPort   what Tor Browser and proxychains connect to explicitly
#   TransPort   transparent proxy, for TCP the firewall redirects here
#   DNSPort     resolver, so name lookups also go through Tor

SocksPort   __GATEWAY_IP__:9050
DNSPort     __GATEWAY_IP__:5353
TransPort   __GATEWAY_IP__:9040

# Make .onion (and .exit) reachable through the transparent path. A .onion name
# has no routable IP, so Tor's resolver maps these suffixes to virtual addresses
# in the range below; a connection to one of those addresses is redirected to
# the TransPort, which recognises it and performs the rendezvous. Without this,
# clearnet works but every onion address fails to resolve — the connection dies
# before it starts. The range is chosen to avoid the lab's own 10.152.x subnets.
AutomapHostsOnResolve 1
AutomapHostsSuffixes .onion,.exit
VirtualAddrNetworkIPv4 10.192.0.0/10

# Control port on the internal interface, so the workstation's apps can reach
# it — Tor Browser to monitor bootstrap and request new circuits, OnionShare to
# publish onion services. They are separate containers now and cannot see this
# container's loopback, which is why it moved off 127.0.0.1. Authentication is a
# hashed password, not a cookie: a cookie would have to be read from a file
# inside this container, which the workstation cannot open. entrypoint.sh fills
# in the auth line below from the shared secret (falling back to cookie auth,
# local only, if none was provided).
ControlPort __GATEWAY_IP__:9051
__CONTROL_AUTH__

# Drop to the unprivileged tor user after binding. The entrypoint installs the
# firewall as root before starting tor, so tor itself needs no privilege.
User tor

DataDirectory /var/lib/tor
Log notice stdout

# No HiddenServiceDir here. Tor refuses to start if one is declared without at
# least one HiddenServicePort, and that failure presents as a container that
# never becomes healthy. Part II adds services as drop-ins.
